{%- comment -%}
  Usage: {% render 'buy-box', product: product, compact: false %}
  compact: true  -> tighter spacing, no product title repeat
  Product-agnostic: every string comes from the product, no hardcoded fallbacks.
  Button says only "Add to Cart" - no price, no spinner. Below 900px the button
  is hidden and the permanent footer bar is the only Add to Cart.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- capture saved_pct -%}{%- render 'saved-percent', variant: product.selected_or_first_available_variant -%}{%- endcapture -%}
{%- liquid
  assign saved_pct = saved_pct | plus: 0
  assign v = product.selected_or_first_available_variant
  assign prepaid_code = settings.prepaid_discount_code
  assign prepaid_pct = settings.prepaid_discount_percent | default: 5

  comment
    The percentage is the offer, so nothing about it may be able to delete the
    offer. That is not hypothetical: it already happened once.

    A previous pass rendered this block only when a computed rupee saving was
    greater than zero. In Liquid, zero is truthy, so `default: 5` does not
    replace a zero the way it replaces nil, and this setting is not written
    into config/settings_data.json at all. A percentage arriving as 0 therefore
    survived the default, multiplied the price to a saving of 0, failed the
    greater-than-zero test, and removed the whole control from the page with no
    error anywhere. A guard meant to prevent an offer reading "save Rs. 0"
    deleted the offer instead.

    So the value is coerced to a number and clamped to the schema's own default
    before anything reads it, and the block below tests only what it actually
    needs: that a code is configured and the variant can be bought. plus: 0
    first because the comparison must be number-to-number — a range setting
    that ever arrives as the string "5" would otherwise be compared against 0
    as text.
  endcomment
  assign prepaid_pct = prepaid_pct | plus: 0
  if prepaid_pct <= 0
    assign prepaid_pct = 5
  endif

  comment
    The urgency line, and the only things it is ever allowed to say.

    "31% OFF, limited-time price" is gone. The percentage was already on the
    price pill and in the struck figure two lines above, so it was the third
    statement of one fact, and "limited-time" was attached to no time — a
    deadline nobody had set and nothing could enforce. On a store selling to
    parents, an urgency claim a customer can catch costs more than the few
    percent it buys.

    What replaced it is the one urgent thing that is actually true and that
    the theme can actually read: the stock figure. An earlier version of this
    comment said no such source existed. That was wrong — Liquid carries
    inventory_quantity whenever the merchant tracks inventory, so this is
    sourced rather than invented, it updates itself as stock moves, and it
    disappears on its own once there is plenty. It is gated on the policy
    being deny, because a variant that oversells is not scarce.

    No countdown, no shopper count, no deadline. Those still have no source.
  endcomment

  assign urgency = ''
  if v.inventory_management != blank and v.inventory_policy == 'deny' and v.inventory_quantity > 0 and v.inventory_quantity <= 10
    assign urgency = 'Only ' | append: v.inventory_quantity | append: ' left in stock'
  elsif settings.dispatch_promise != blank
    assign urgency = settings.dispatch_promise
  endif
-%}
<div class="buybox {% if compact %}buybox--compact{% endif %}" data-buybox id="buybox">

  {%- unless compact -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      No product.type eyebrow. It printed "PLAY MAT" directly above "MamaJoy
      Baby Play Mat – Toys & Cars", so it repeated a word the heading already
      carried and cost 36px of the one screen where the name and the price
      both have to be readable. A category label earns its place when it says
      something the title does not; this one did not.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <h1 class="buybox-title">{{ product.title }}</h1>
    {%- comment -%}
      Evidence sits between the name and the price, the order the questions
      actually get asked in: what is it, is it any good, what does it cost.
      Same snippet and same scope as the product card, so it speaks only for
      this product and renders nothing at all until it has a review of its own
      to report — which is the state every product is in today.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {% render 'product-rating', product: product %}
  {%- endunless -%}

  <div class="buybox-price-row">
    {% render 'price', variant: v %}
    <span class="buybox-tax-note">Inclusive of taxes</span>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    Under the price, above the offer. This is the answer to "why now", and it
    belongs after the shopper knows the number and before they are asked to
    act on it — a reason to hurry that arrives after the button has already
    been offered is a nag rather than a reason.

    Red, and the only red on the page. --danger is otherwise reserved for
    things that have gone wrong, which is exactly what earns it attention
    here; it is 6.06:1 on chalk, so it passes as body text and not merely as a
    decoration. One line, 13px, medium — a step below the price it qualifies
    and two below the button it points at. It does not blink, count down,
    animate or fill a box, because every one of those reads as a marketplace
    listing rather than as a brand, and this store's whole argument is that it
    is not one.

    It says nothing at all when there is nothing true to say. No sale on the
    variant and no dispatch promise entered means no element, no empty space
    and no placeholder.

    Full buy box only. The compact one already drops the title and the rating;
    on the PDP it is the closing module, and the same red line twice on one
    page is how a true statement starts sounding like a technique.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if urgency != blank -%}
    {%- unless compact -%}
      <p class="buybox-urgency">{{ urgency }}</p>
    {%- endunless -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- form 'product', product, data-buybox-form: '' -%}
    <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ v.id }}" data-variant-id>

    {%- comment -%}
      The offer, before the quantity rather than under the button. A shopper
      decides how to pay while they are still deciding what to pay, and an
      incentive introduced after the primary action has already been offered is
      an upsell; introduced before it, it is a reason.

      It leads with "Prepay", because that is the condition attached to the
      saving and a shopper who reads the discount before the condition has to
      re-read the line. "an additional" is the other half of the same job: the
      price above already carries a discount, and without that word this reads
      as restating it rather than as a second one stacked on top.

      The figure is the configured percentage, printed straight. Nothing here
      derives a rupee amount any more, so there is no rounding to disagree with
      what checkout charges, and no arithmetic that can fail quietly and take
      the control down with it.

      One tap really is one tap: the shared handler adds the item, then sends
      the shopper to /discount/CODE?redirect=/checkout, which is Shopify's own
      mechanism for applying a code without the shopper typing it. The code is
      shown rather than hidden behind the offer, because a saving whose shape a
      shopper cannot see reads as a trick.

      Below 900px this is the only visible button in the panel, so leaving for
      checkout has to be disclosed rather than sprung. The arrow does that for
      anyone who can see it; the sentence after the code does it for anyone who
      cannot, which is why the caption is four words on screen and a full one
      in the accessible name.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The prepaid button is removed, and this is the note explaining why so it
      is not reinstated by someone reading the settings and assuming it was
      dropped by accident.

      It navigated to /discount/CODE?redirect=/checkout. That endpoint does not
      preview a saving — it writes the code onto the shopper's cart, and
      Shopify keeps it there for the rest of the session. One tap and every
      later read of /cart.js comes back 5% lower: on this page, in the drawer,
      on the cart page and at checkout, whether or not the shopper ever pays
      online. Nothing in the flow ever took it back off, so a customer who
      tapped it and then chose cash on delivery still got the online-payment
      price, and the drawer showed a total 5% under the lines above it with
      nothing on screen accounting for the gap.

      An offer that cannot be declined once accepted is not an offer, and the
      default state of the cart has to be the price on the product.

      Nothing else was deleted. The CARE5 and PREPAY5 codes are still ACTIVE in
      the admin, the settings (prepaid_discount_code, _label, _percent) are
      untouched, the styles are still in component-buy-box.css and the handler
      is still in theme.js. Restoring this is putting the markup back — but
      whatever replaces it needs a way to remove the code again, or it brings
      the same bug with it.
    {%- endcomment -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      The offer, between the price and the quantity, which is where the old
      prepaid button sat and for the same reason: it is information about this
      purchase, and it belongs beside the decision rather than under the button
      that ends it.

      What is different is the only thing that mattered. This displays a code
      and copies it. It does not navigate to /discount/, it does not touch the
      cart, and it does not run before, during or after Add to Cart. The old
      control wrote the code onto the shopper's cart the moment it was tapped
      and nothing could take it back off, so a customer who changed their mind
      still paid the online-payment price. A code on the clipboard commits the
      shopper to nothing — they type it at checkout if they want it, and the
      cart keeps the price on the product until they do.

      type="button" is load-bearing: this markup renders inside the product
      form, and a default-type button inside a form submits it. The copy
      control would have become a second Add to Cart.

      Code and percentage come from the existing settings rather than new ones.
      The ids still say "prepaid" because renaming a setting id orphans the
      value the merchant has already saved; the offer they configure is the
      offer that shows.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if prepaid_code != blank -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Chalk on a chalk page, bounded by a hairline — the store's own
        chalk-surface pattern, lifted verbatim from the product card
        (component-product-card.css) rather than mixed fresh. That is what
        makes this read as part of the storefront instead of a component
        pasted onto it, and it is why the border is here: on a chalk page a
        chalk fill is invisible without one. The border draws the surface; the
        hierarchy is still carried by type.

        The copy names a reason rather than announcing a percentage. It does
        not say "first order" or "new here", because CARE5 is not restricted
        to either — allCustomers, no per-customer limit — and copy that
        promises a condition the discount does not enforce is a lie the
        customer discovers at checkout.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="offer">
        <p class="offer-text">Enjoy {{ prepaid_pct }}% off with <code class="offer-code" data-offer-code>{{ prepaid_code }}</code></p>
        <button type="button" class="offer-copy" data-offer-copy data-offer-value="{{ prepaid_code }}">
          <span class="offer-copy-idle">Copy code</span>
          <span class="offer-copy-done" aria-hidden="true">Copied</span>
          <span class="visually-hidden">Copy discount code {{ prepaid_code }} to the clipboard</span>
        </button>
        <span class="visually-hidden" role="status" data-offer-status></span>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    <div class="qty-row">
      <span class="qty-label" id="qtyLabel-{{ section.id }}">Quantity</span>
      <div class="stepper" data-qty-stepper>
        <button type="button" class="qty-btn" data-qty-minus aria-label="Decrease quantity">{% render 'icon', name: 'minus', size: 16 %}</button>
        <input type="number" class="qty-input" name="quantity" value="1" min="1" max="10" step="1" inputmode="numeric" data-qty-input aria-labelledby="qtyLabel-{{ section.id }}">
        <button type="button" class="qty-btn" data-qty-plus aria-label="Increase quantity">{% render 'icon', name: 'plus', size: 16 %}</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      Same pending pattern as the product card: both words ship and CSS shows
      one, keyed off the aria-busy the shared handler sets. No spinner.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <button type="submit" class="cta cta-block" data-add-to-cart {% unless v.available %}disabled{% endunless %}>
      {%- if v.available -%}
        <span class="btn-label">Add to Cart</span>
        <span class="btn-pending" aria-hidden="true">Adding&hellip;</span>
      {%- else -%}Sold out{%- endif -%}
    </button>

    <p class="buybox-error" data-buybox-error role="alert" hidden></p>
  {%- endform -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Four facts, each one true and each one already promised elsewhere on the
    site: the announcement bar, the FAQ and the shipping accordion all say the
    same things. No countdown, no visitor count, no invented stock figure —
    the only urgency here is the kind that survives a shopper checking it.

    A list rather than a sentence, because these are four separate claims and a
    screen reader should be able to count them. The tick is the theme's own
    icon, marked decorative: the words already carry the meaning and a repeated
    "check" announcement four times over is noise.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <ul class="buybox-trust">
    <li>{% render 'icon', name: 'check', size: 14 %}Cash on delivery</li>
    <li>{% render 'icon', name: 'check', size: 14 %}Free shipping</li>
    <li>{% render 'icon', name: 'check', size: 14 %}30-day returns</li>
    <li>{% render 'icon', name: 'check', size: 14 %}Secure checkout</li>
  </ul>
</div>
